  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThis very active weather pattern will continue throughout the\nweekend, finally coming to an end after a cold front passes\nMonday evening.\n\nA large area of high pressure remains locked over the southern\nstates. On the northern periphery of the ridge, westerly mid- \nlevel flow will continue over the Midwest and Middle Ohio\nValley, bringing rounds of vorticity and low-level moisture \ninto our area, triggering the daily development of showers and \nthunderstorms. \n\nThe atmosphere will remain very moist all weekend with \nprecipitable water values ranging from 2.00 to 2.25 inches \nacross the region. Dew point temperatures will be in the middle \n70s each day through Monday. Any showers and thunderstorms that \ndevelop will have the potential of producing torrential \ndownpours. Given the saturated soils, especially across \nsoutheast Ohio, any downpours will have the potential of \nproducing flash flooding, especially if multiple thunderstorms \ntrack over the same locations. Given recent high resolution \nmodel runs and rainfall totals from the past several days, the \nprime locations for flooding will most likely be across \nsoutheast Ohio and northern and central West Virginia.\n\nDrier weather will finally return Tuesday in the wake of a large\ncold front. Dry weather with less humidity is expected Tuesday\nand Wednesday with high pressure overhead. Another system may\napproach Thursday, bringing additional shower and thunderstorm\nchances.",
